\begin{abstract}
We give a rigorous, self-contained treatment of four structural probes of a semiprime $N=pq$ ($p\neq q$ prime), each proposed as a route to a factor-revealing witness lying outside the known classification of numeric witnesses, and we close all four.
First, the \emph{agreement set} $A(N)=\{a\in\units{N}:\legsym{a}{p}=\legsym{a}{q}\}$, invariant under both the swap $p\leftrightarrow q$ and conjugation, satisfies $2|A(N)|=\varphi(N)$ exactly (for odd $p$) and coincides with the level set $\{a: J(a\mid N)=1\}$ of the $N$-computable Jacobi symbol; the aggregate collapses onto the quadratic character modulo $N$.
Second, the \emph{zero-divisor graph} of $\Zmod{N}$ is determined completely: its vertex set splits into wings of sizes $q-1$ and $p-1$, adjacency holds exactly across wings, so the graph is $K_{q-1,p-1}$; hence $|V|=p+q-2$ and $\deg(x)+1$ is a prime factor of $N$ for every vertex $x$. This structural witness collapses onto the numeric trace witness and is circular: exhibiting one vertex already exhibits a factor, since $\gcd(x,N)\in\{p,q\}$.
Third, we prove a \emph{noise-floor bound for atomic uniform primitives}: a single uniform query on $[1,N)$ reveals a factor with probability $(p+q-2)/N\le 2/\min(p,q)$, minimized near $2/\sqrt{N}$ at balanced semiprimes; by contrast, the \emph{correlated} sample set of the static rho map $x\mapsto x^2+1$ provably contains a factor-bearing pair among its first $p+1$ iterates, unconditionally, by reduction-compatibility and pigeonhole. The density gain is real and is paid for exactly by the aggregation cost $\binom{p+1}{2}\ge p$.
Fourth, the linearized digit convolution admits an explicit constant-norm kernel vector, the carry commutator $C=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)$; consequently every factorization target has a non-rank-one companion solution at squared distance at most $8$, uniformly in $N$, so short-vector search cannot isolate the factorization.
We close with a tropical reformulation in which the divisor hyperbola degenerates to a min-plus line whose corner sits at $\sqrt{N}$, straddled by every divisor pair.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} semiprime; Legendre symbol; Jacobi symbol; character orthogonality; zero-divisor graph; complete bipartite graph; Pollard rho; pigeonhole; lattice relaxation; Gaussian heuristic; tropical geometry.

\section{Introduction}

Let $N=pq$ with $p\neq q$ prime. A \emph{witness} for $N$ is any datum from which $p$ and $q$ can be recovered in time polynomial in $\log N$. Classical examples are numeric: the trace $p+q$; the larger factor; the value $\varphi(N)=(p-1)(q-1)$; the order of the unit group; a nontrivial square root of $1$ modulo $N$. All are equivalent to the factorization by elementary algebra: from $s=p+q$ and $N=pq$ one solves $X^2-sX+N=0$; from $\varphi(N)$ one recovers $s=N+1-\varphi(N)$.

The interesting question is whether there exist witnesses of a genuinely different type---objects not of this classified numeric kind, whose evaluation might evade the barriers blocking the numeric ones. Four candidates are studied here.

\begin{enumerate}
\item[\textbf{(A)}] \emph{Symmetric character aggregates.} A quantity computable from $N$ alone is necessarily invariant under $p\leftrightarrow q$. This suggests searching for a probe that \emph{is} invariant under all available symmetries and yet separates semiprimes of equal size. The agreement set of Legendre symbols is the canonical candidate.
\item[\textbf{(B)}] \emph{Structural (non-numeric) witnesses.} A graph attached to $\Zmod{N}$ is not a number; if its isomorphism class determines $\{p,q\}$, it is a witness outside the letter of the numeric classification.
\item[\textbf{(C)}] \emph{Correlated samplers versus the noise floor.} A heuristic ``noise-floor principle'' asserts that factor-bearing events have density at most $\approx N^{-1/2}$. The static rho sample set measurably violates the density form of this statement.
\item[\textbf{(D)}] \emph{Geometry of numbers on the digits.} Digit multiplication is a convolution; linearizing it produces a lattice built from the digits of $N$ alone.
\end{enumerate}

We resolve all four. Each resolution is negative for factoring but positive as mathematics: each is an exact structural theorem about $\Zmod{N}$, valid for all semiprimes.

\section{Notation}

Throughout $p\neq q$ are primes and $N=pq$. We write $\units{N}$ for the unit group, of order $\varphi(N)=(p-1)(q-1)$; $\pi_p:\Zmod{N}\to\Zmod{p}$ and $\pi_q:\Zmod{N}\to\Zmod{q}$ for the reduction homomorphisms, which by the Chinese Remainder Theorem combine to an isomorphism onto the product; $\legsym{a}{p}\in\{0,\pm1\}$ for the Legendre symbol, abbreviating the symbol of $\pi_p(a)$ when $a$ is a residue mod $N$; and $J(a\mid N)$ for the Jacobi symbol, which for $N=pq$ equals $\legsym{a}{p}\legsym{a}{q}$ and is computable in $O(\log^2 N)$ bit operations from $a$ and $N$ alone. If $a$ is a unit mod $N$ then $\pi_p(a)$ is a unit mod $p$, so $\legsym{a}{p}\in\{\pm1\}$; likewise at $q$. We use this dichotomy without further comment.

\section{Closure A: symmetric character aggregates collapse}

\begin{definition}[Agreement set]
For $N=pq$,
\[
A(N)=\bigl\{\,a\in\units{N} \;:\; \legsym{a}{p}=\legsym{a}{q}\,\bigr\}.
\]
\end{definition}

$A(N)$ is invariant under the swap $p\leftrightarrow q$ and under conjugation, hence a legitimate candidate for a both-symmetries-invariant aggregate.

\begin{lemma}[Flipping unit]\label{lem:flip}
Let $p$ be an odd prime and $q\neq p$ prime. There exists $u\in\units{N}$ with $\legsym{u}{p}=-1$ and $\legsym{u}{q}=+1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Since $p$ is odd, the quadratic character of $\Zmod{p}$ is nontrivial, so there is $y\in\Zmod{p}$ with $\legsym{y}{p}=-1$; in particular $y\neq0$. As $p\neq q$ are primes they are coprime, so by the Chinese Remainder Theorem there is an integer $k$ with $k\equiv y\pmod p$ and $k\equiv 1\pmod q$. Then $p\nmid k$ (else $y=0$) and $q\nmid k$ (else $1\equiv0\bmod q$), so $\gcd(k,N)=1$ and $k$ defines a unit $u$. By construction $\pi_p(u)=y$ and $\pi_q(u)=1$, giving the two symbol values.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Translation exchanges agreement and disagreement]\label{lem:exchange}
With $u$ as in Lemma~\ref{lem:flip}, for every unit $a$ we have $au\in A(N)\iff a\notin A(N)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By multiplicativity, $\legsym{au}{p}=-\legsym{a}{p}$ and $\legsym{au}{q}=\legsym{a}{q}$. Since both symbols lie in $\{\pm1\}$, the equality $-\legsym{a}{p}=\legsym{a}{q}$ holds precisely when $\legsym{a}{p}\neq\legsym{a}{q}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Agreement collapse]\label{thm:agree}
Let $p$ be an odd prime, $q\neq p$ prime, $N=pq$. Then
\[
2\,|A(N)| = \varphi(N) = (p-1)(q-1),
\]
i.e.\ $|A(N)|=\varphi(N)/2$ exactly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Right multiplication by $u$ is a bijection of $\units{N}$, and by Lemma~\ref{lem:exchange} it carries $A(N)$ onto its complement in $\units{N}$. Hence $|A(N)|=|A(N)^{c}|$, and since the two sets partition a group of order $\varphi(N)$, $2|A(N)|=\varphi(N)$. The explicit value follows from multiplicativity of $\varphi$ on coprime arguments.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Agreement is a Jacobi level set]\label{thm:jacobi}
For every unit $a$ of $\Zmod{N}$ with representative $\tilde a\in\{0,\dots,N-1\}$,
\[
a\in A(N)\iff J(\tilde a\mid N)=1 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The Legendre symbol of $\pi_p(a)$ equals that of the integer $\tilde a$ at $p$, and likewise at $q$. By multiplicativity of the Jacobi symbol in its lower argument, $J(\tilde a\mid pq)=\legsym{\tilde a}{p}\legsym{\tilde a}{q}$. Both factors lie in $\{\pm1\}$, so their product is $+1$ exactly when they are equal.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Membership in $A(N)$ is decidable in $O(\log^2 N)$ bit operations from $N$ and $a$ alone; the aggregate carries no information about $\{p,q\}$ beyond what $N$ already determines.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
This is character orthogonality in action. Writing $\chi_p,\chi_q$ for the two pulled-back quadratic characters,
$\mathbf{1}[\chi_p(a)=\chi_q(a)]=\tfrac12\bigl(1+\chi_p(a)\chi_q(a)\bigr)$, and $\chi_p\chi_q$ is the public Jacobi character mod $N$. Summing over $a$ and using $\sum_a \chi_p(a)\chi_q(a)=0$ reproves Theorem~\ref{thm:agree} in one line.
\end{remark}

\section{Closure B: the zero-divisor graph is complete bipartite, and circular}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{zero-divisor graph} $\Gamma(N)$ has vertex set $V(N)=\{x: 0<x<N,\ \gcd(x,N)\neq1\}$, with $x\sim y$ iff $x\neq y$ and $N\mid xy$. For $d\mid N$ the \emph{wing} of $d$ is $W_d=\{x: 0<x<N,\ d\mid x\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Wing decomposition]\label{prop:wings}
$V(N)=W_p\cup W_q$ and $W_p\cap W_q=\emptyset$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
If $\gcd(x,N)>1$ then some prime $r$ divides $x$ and $N=pq$, so $r\in\{p,q\}$. Conversely a nonzero multiple of $p$ below $N$ has $\gcd$ with $N$ divisible by $p$. For disjointness: $p\mid x$ and $q\mid x$ with $p,q$ coprime give $N\mid x$, so $x\ge N$, contradicting $x<N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Wing sizes]\label{prop:sizes}
$|W_p|=q-1$, $|W_q|=p-1$, and hence $|V(N)|=p+q-2$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$k\mapsto pk$ is a bijection from $\{1,\dots,q-1\}$ onto $W_p$ (injective by cancellation, surjective since every multiple of $p$ in $(0,N)$ is $pk$ with $0<k<q$). Symmetrically for $W_q$; the wings are disjoint, so the counts add.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bipartite structure]\label{thm:bipartite}
For $x,y\in V(N)$, $x\sim y$ if and only if $x$ and $y$ lie in \emph{different} wings. Equivalently $\Gamma(N)\cong K_{q-1,\,p-1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $x=pa$, $y=qb$ then $xy=pq\,ab$, so $N\mid xy$, and $x\neq y$ by disjointness of the wings. Conversely, suppose $x,y\in W_p$ and $N\mid xy$. Then $q\mid xy$, and $q$ prime gives $q\mid x$ or $q\mid y$; that element then lies in $W_p\cap W_q=\emptyset$, a contradiction. The case $x,y\in W_q$ is symmetric. By Proposition~\ref{prop:wings} every vertex lies in a wing, so cross-wing pairs are the only edges.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Degrees; factor recovery]\label{cor:degrees}
Every $x\in W_p$ has $\deg(x)=|W_q|=p-1$ and every $x\in W_q$ has $\deg(x)=|W_p|=q-1$. Hence for every vertex $x$,
\[
\deg(x)+1\in\{p,q\}\quad\text{and}\quad(\deg(x)+1)\mid N .
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Collapse onto the trace]
Set $s=|V(N)|+2$. Then $s=p+q$ and both primes are roots of $X^2-sX+N=0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Proposition~\ref{prop:sizes} gives $s=p+q$; then $p^2-(p+q)p+pq=0$ and likewise for $q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Circularity]\label{thm:circular}
For every $x\in V(N)$ we have $\gcd(x,N)\in\{p,q\}$. Exhibiting a single vertex therefore already exhibits a nontrivial factor.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $x\in W_p$ (the other case is symmetric) and $g=\gcd(x,N)$. Then $p\mid g$; write $g=pk$. From $g\mid pq$ and cancellation, $k\mid q$, so $k\in\{1,q\}$. If $k=q$ then $q\mid g\mid x$, placing $x\in W_p\cap W_q=\emptyset$. Hence $g=p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The vertex set has density $(p+q-2)/N$ in $[1,N)$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:atomic} below, locating a vertex by uniform search succeeds with probability at most $2/\min(p,q)$ per query. Thus \emph{building} $\Gamma(N)$ costs $\Omega(\min(p,q))$ queries, while \emph{reading} it is free. The structural witness is real, lies outside the numeric classification, and is nonetheless sealed by aggregation cost together with circularity.
\end{corollary}

\section{Closure C: scoping the noise-floor principle}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{hit density} of $N=pq$ is $\delta(N)=|V(N)|/N=(p+q-2)/(pq)$: the probability that a single uniform draw $a\leftarrow[1,N)$ satisfies $\gcd(a,N)>1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Atomic uniform noise-floor bound]\label{thm:atomic}
If $p\le q$ then $\delta(N)\le 2/p=2/\min(p,q)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$p+q-2<2q$, so $\delta(N)=(p+q-2)/(pq)<2q/(pq)=2/p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The floor and its extremal case]
$(p+q)^2\ge 4pq$ for all $p,q$, with equality iff $p=q$. Consequently $(\delta(N)N+2)^2\ge4N$, i.e.\ $\delta(N)\ge(2\sqrt N-2)/N$: the density is minimized, at $\approx 2/\sqrt N$, exactly at balanced semiprimes.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$(p+q)^2-4pq=(p-q)^2\ge0$, vanishing iff $p=q$. Since $\delta(N)N=p+q-2$, we get $(\delta(N)N+2)^2=(p+q)^2\ge4pq=4N$.
\end{proof}

We now contrast the \emph{derived} sample set of a polynomial iteration. Let $\rho(x)=x^2+1$ over $\mathbb{Z}$ and $x_t=\rho^{[t]}(x_0)$.

\begin{lemma}[Reduction-compatibility]
For any modulus $m$ and $a\equiv b\pmod m$: $\rho(a)\equiv\rho(b)\pmod m$, and by induction $\rho^{[t]}(a)\equiv\rho^{[t]}(b)\pmod m$ for all $t\ge0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Congruence is preserved by multiplication and addition, giving the one-step claim; induct on $t$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unconditional collision]\label{thm:collision}
For any $p\ge1$ and any $x_0\in\mathbb{Z}$, there exist $i<j\le p$ with $p\mid x_j-x_i$. No probabilistic hypothesis is used.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The map $t\mapsto x_t\bmod p$ on the $p+1$ indices $\{0,\dots,p\}$ takes values in a set of size $p$, so by pigeonhole it is not injective; order a coincident pair as $i<j$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Extraction]\label{lem:extract}
If $p,q$ are primes, $d\in\mathbb{Z}$, $p\mid d$ and $q\nmid d$, then $\gcd(d,pq)=p$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Let $g=\gcd(d,pq)$. Then $p\mid g$; write $g=pk$ with $k\mid q$ by cancellation, so $k\in\{1,q\}$. If $k=q$ then $q\mid g\mid d$, contradicting $q\nmid d$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Correctness core of the rho method]
For any $x_0$ and distinct primes $p,q$, there exist $i<j\le p$ with $p\mid x_j-x_i$; and for any such pair with $q\nmid x_j-x_i$ we have $\gcd(x_j-x_i,N)=p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:collision} and Lemma~\ref{lem:extract}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Aggregation cost]
For every $p\ge0$, $\binom{p+1}{2}=p(p+1)/2\ge p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Scoping]
Extracting a factor from the guaranteed collision by unstructured pairwise gcds requires $\binom{T}{2}$ operations with $T=p+1$, hence at least $p$ operations---no fewer than trial division up to $p$. The density gain of the correlated sample set is therefore not a complexity gain.
\end{corollary}

The correct statement of the principle is consequently: the bound $\Pr[\text{success}]\le 2/\min(p,q)$ applies to an \emph{atomic uniform primitive}---a single query from the uniform distribution on $[1,N)$ or a distribution close to it. It is \emph{not} a density theorem about derived or correlated sample sets. Correlated sets may have far higher factor-bearing density; what they do not evade is the cost of the aggregation that converts density into a factor. The genuine $\tilde O(\sqrt p)$ running time of the rho method arises from replacing exhaustive pairwise comparison by cycle detection together with a birthday heuristic---a known method, and the recognized exception.

\section{Closure D: the digit relaxation is degenerate}

Fix a base $b\ge2$ and two-digit factors $p=u_0+u_1b$, $q=v_0+v_1b$. Introducing $w_{ij}=u_iv_j$ linearizes the digit equations, with the number recovered by the \emph{digit functional}
\[
\Phi_b(w)=\sum_{i,j\in\{0,1\}} w_{ij}\,b^{\,i+j}.
\]
Write $\langle u\rangle_b=u_0+u_1b$, let $u\otimes v=(u_iv_j)_{i,j}$, and let $\norm{w}^2=\sum_{i,j}w_{ij}^2$.

\begin{proposition}[Faithfulness]
$\Phi_b(u\otimes v)=\langle u\rangle_b\,\langle v\rangle_b$; hence rank-one solutions of $\Phi_b(w)=N$ with admissible digits are exactly the factorizations of $N$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[The discarded constraint]
$\det(u\otimes v)=(u_0v_0)(u_1v_1)-(u_0v_1)(u_1v_0)=0$. Rank-one matrices are exactly those killed by the determinant, a \emph{quadratic} function that the linear relaxation cannot express.
\end{proposition}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{carry commutator} is $C=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Kernel vector of constant norm]\label{thm:kernel}
For every base $b$: $\Phi_b(C)=b^{0+1}-b^{1+0}=0$ and $\norm{C}^2=2$.
\end{theorem}

The reason is structural: the coefficient $b^{i+j}$ depends only on $i+j$, so the functional cannot distinguish the transposed positions $(0,1)$ and $(1,0)$. The information lost in that identification is exactly the carry information of the original digit multiplication.

\begin{theorem}[Non-isolation of the factorization target]\label{thm:noniso}
For every base $b$ and all digit vectors $u,v$ there exists an integer matrix $w$ with
\[
\Phi_b(w)=\Phi_b(u\otimes v),\qquad \det w\neq0,\qquad \norm{w-u\otimes v}^2\le 8 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $w=u\otimes v+cC$ with $c\in\{1,2\}$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:kernel}, $\Phi_b(w)=\Phi_b(u\otimes v)$, and $\norm{w-u\otimes v}^2=c^2\norm{C}^2=2c^2\le8$. A direct $2\times2$ computation with $D=u_0v_1-u_1v_0$ gives $\det(u\otimes v+cC)=cD+c^2$. If $D+1\neq0$ take $c=1$, giving determinant $D+1\neq0$; otherwise $D=-1$ and $c=2$ gives determinant $-2+4=2\neq0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
$\norm{u\otimes v}^2=(u_0^2+u_1^2)(v_0^2+v_1^2)$; if all four digits are nonzero this is at least $4$, and it grows multiplicatively with the digit norms of $p$ and $q$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{corollary}
The relaxed solution set contains, within an $O(1)$-radius ball around every factorization target, spurious solutions of the same digit value with nonzero determinant. Since the target's own norm grows with $N$ while the perturbation stays of constant size, the factorization is never an isolated shortest vector, and a reduction algorithm returning a (nearly) shortest vector has no mechanism to prefer the rank-one point. This is the exact structural content of the observation that the target sits \emph{at} the Gaussian heuristic $\sqrt{\dim}$ rather than well below it. Re-imposing rank one together with carry integrality is not a repair: it is the $\Theta(N)$-state de-carrying dynamic program, i.e.\ the original problem restated.
\end{corollary}

\section{The tropical picture: the corner at $\sqrt N$}

The divisor locus $xy=N$ becomes, in logarithmic coordinates, the line $X+Y=\log N$. In the min-plus semiring, where $a\oplus b=\min(a,b)$ and $a\odot b=a+b$, the tropical line $X\odot Y=\log N$ has its corner at $X=Y=\tfrac12\log N$, i.e.\ at $\sqrt N$.

\begin{proposition}[Corner straddle]
If $p\le q$ and $N=pq$ then $p^2\le N\le q^2$; in logarithmic coordinates $\log p\le\tfrac12\log N\le\log q$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Tropical submultiplicativity of the integer logarithm]
For primes $p,q$, $\lfloor\log_2 p\rfloor\odot\lfloor\log_2 q\rfloor\le\lfloor\log_2(pq)\rfloor$, i.e.\ $\lfloor\log_2 p\rfloor+\lfloor\log_2 q\rfloor\le\lfloor\log_2 N\rfloor$; and the tropical sum (minimum) of the two coordinates is at most the corner coordinate.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$2^{\lfloor\log_2 p\rfloor}2^{\lfloor\log_2 q\rfloor}\le pq$, so the exponent sum is at most $\lfloor\log_2(pq)\rfloor$; the second claim is the straddle in logarithmic form.
\end{proof}

The corner is the geometric form of the noise floor: the balanced case $p\approx q\approx\sqrt N$ is where the hit density attains its minimum $\approx 2/\sqrt N$. Each of the four probes is, in this language, an attempt to reach the corner along a path that does not cross the $\sqrt N$ scale.

\section{Algorithms and costs}

Let $n=\log_2 N$.

\paragraph{Agreement test.} Given $N,a$, return $[\,J(a\mid N)=1\,]$ by the reciprocity-based Euclidean algorithm, at cost $O(n^2)$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:jacobi} this decides membership in $A(N)$ without knowing $p,q$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:agree} the count is $\varphi(N)/2$, so no query strategy against $A(N)$ separates two semiprimes of equal $\varphi$-value.

\paragraph{Zero-divisor graph reader.} Given a vertex $x$, output $\gcd(x,N)$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:circular}) or $\deg(x)+1$ (Corollary~\ref{cor:degrees}); cost $O(n^2)$ given the vertex, while producing the vertex costs $\Omega(\min(p,q))$ expected queries.

\paragraph{Unconditional rho collision.} Iterate $x\mapsto x^2+1\bmod N$ for $T=p+1$ steps and test all pairs; success is guaranteed, at $\Theta(p^2)$ gcds. Floyd cycle detection reduces this to $O(T)$ gcds but reintroduces the birthday heuristic $T\approx\sqrt p$.

\paragraph{Digit-lattice perturbation certificate.} Given $b$ and a target $u\otimes v$, output $w=u\otimes v+cC$ with $c\in\{1,2\}$ chosen so that $cD+c^2\neq0$, $D=u_0v_1-u_1v_0$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:noniso} this is a same-value, non-rank-one lattice point at squared distance at most $8$, produced in $O(1)$ operations.

\section{Discussion}

The four closures fail for four genuinely different reasons: \emph{symmetry saturation} (an aggregate invariant under all symmetries available to a $p,q$-agnostic observer becomes computable by that observer); \emph{access rather than information} (the zero-divisor graph contains the factorization redundantly, but its vertex set coincides with the divisor structure); \emph{scope of a probabilistic bound} (a bound proved for one sampling model was applied to another, and the repair sharpens the picture); and \emph{relaxation degeneracy} (linearizing a rank constraint creates a kernel containing a constant-norm lattice vector).

None of these results is a lower bound on the complexity of integer factorization. Theorem~\ref{thm:agree} does not preclude a non-symmetric aggregate being useful; Theorem~\ref{thm:bipartite} does not preclude other structural witnesses, only shows that this one is circular; Theorem~\ref{thm:atomic} bounds a single query; Theorem~\ref{thm:noniso} addresses the specific two-digit relaxation with the functional $\Phi_b$.

A common ledger runs through all four. Each candidate satisfies one of: (i) information content zero; (ii) evaluation cost at least $\min(p,q)$; (iii) target not isolated. The agreement set falls in (i), the zero-divisor graph and the rho sample set in (ii), the digit lattice in (iii). No candidate here lies in the desired regime of positive information at polylogarithmic cost.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Structural-witness dichotomy]
For distinct primes $p\neq q$, any graph invariant of the zero-divisor graph of $\Zmod{pq}$ computable in time $\mathrm{polylog}\,N$ from $N$ alone is a function of $N$ alone; equivalently, every invariant separating two semiprimes of equal size requires $\Omega(\min(p,q))$ vertex queries.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Atomic-uniform noise floor as a theorem]
Let $D$ be a distribution on $[1,N)$ whose density is computable from $N$ in $\mathrm{polylog}\,N$ time and which is $\varepsilon$-close to uniform in total variation. Then a single sample from $D$ reveals a nontrivial factor with probability at most $2/\min(p,q)+\varepsilon$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Aggregation lower bound for correlated samplers]
Any algorithm factoring $N=pq$ using only pairwise gcds of iterates of a degree-$d$ polynomial map mod $N$ must perform $\Omega(\sqrt p)$ gcd operations, and the $p+1$ pigeonhole bound is tight up to the birthday square root.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Lattice non-isolation in all dimensions]
For digit vectors of any length $m$, the kernel of $\Phi_b$ on $m\times m$ matrices contains the antisymmetric transposition matrices $E_{ij}-E_{ji}$ with $i+j$ fixed, each of squared norm $2$; hence the factorization target is non-isolated in every dimension, and no linear relaxation of the digit convolution can isolate the factorization, for any base and digit length.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

The agreement set of Legendre symbols has exactly $\varphi(N)/2$ elements and is the Jacobi level set, so symmetric character aggregates collapse. The zero-divisor graph of $\Zmod{pq}$ is exactly $K_{q-1,p-1}$, with $p+q-2$ vertices and degrees giving the factors---yet a single vertex already gives a factor by one gcd. The atomic uniform sampling primitive succeeds with probability $(p+q-2)/N\le2/\min(p,q)$, minimized near $2/\sqrt N$, while the correlated rho sample set provably contains a factor-bearing pair among its first $p+1$ elements, a genuine density escape paid for exactly by the $\binom{p+1}{2}\ge p$ aggregation cost. The linearized digit convolution admits a constant-norm kernel vector, placing a non-factorization solution within squared distance $8$ of every target. Information about the factorization is everywhere in the structure of $\Zmod{N}$, and it is everywhere either symmetry-saturated, circular, aggregation-bound, or non-isolated.

